ulse quality | Best use |\n102: |---|---|---|\n103: | **R (Regular)** | **Best — sharpest CSF pulse** | **Meal bolus + pre-bed** |\n104: | I (NPH) | Good — softer, delayed | Daytime basal replacement only |\n105: | H (Humalog) | Very sharp — fastest | Correction only |\n106: | T (Tresiba) | **None — flat, no pulse** | Basal safety floor only |\n107: \n108: ### Personal Evidence\n109: \n110: | Observation | Pulsatility explanation |\n111: |---|---|\n112: | Pre-bed R → mental 7.5 on <3h sleep | Single R pulse → CSF peak during REM |\n113: | Fasting days → mental 2.5–4 | Zero meal pulses → brain starved |\n114: | CNY 40u felt better than March 22u | 3–4 daily meal pulses vs fasting |\n115: | Agitation/violent imagery at normal BG | Brain insulin pulsatility gap — inject 2R immediately |\n116: | Back-sleep worsens mental scores | CAR disruption + missed morning pulse |\n117: \n118: ### The Deepest Implication\n119: \n120: > **Mental health symptoms in T1D — depression, cognitive fog, fatigue, agitation — are not purely psychiatric. They are partially a neurological insulin-signaling deficiency that no psychiatric medication directly addresses. The substrate (brain glucose stability + insulin pulsatility) must be fixed first.**\n121: \n122: ### Practical Target: 3–4 Daily Brain Pulses\n123: \n124: | Time | Injection | CSF peak | Brain benefit |\n125: |---|---|---|---|\n126: | 12:00–13:00 | 4–6R with lunch | ~15:00–17:00 | Pulse 1 — afternoon concentration |\n127: | 17:00–19:00 | 3–5R with dinner | ~19:00–21:00 | Pulse 2 — evening mood stability |\n128: | **20:30–21:00** | **2R bridge** | **~23:00–01:00** | **Pulse 3 — prevent 4h evening gap** |\n129: | **23:00** | **2–3R pre-bed** | **~01:00–03:00** | **Pulse 4 — most critical: deep sleep + memory consolidation** |\n130: \n131: **Maximum 2h gap between R injections in the evening.** Agitation, inability to concentrate, and violent imagery at normal BG = pulsatility gap signal → inject 2R immediately.\n132: \n133: ---\n134: \n135: ## Daily Insulin Protocol Target\n136: \n137: | Time | Injection | Purpose |\n138: |---|---|---|\n139: | 07:00–09:00 | 5–6T (split two sites) | Basal floor — dawn coverage |\n140: | 12:00–14:00 | 4–6R with meal | Brain pulse 1 + meal bolus |\n141: | 15:00–16:00 | 3–4T (afternoon split) | Bridge overnight onset gap |\n142: | 17:00–19:00 | 3–5R with dinner | Brain pulse 2 + meal bolus |\n143: | 20:30–21:00 | 2R bridge | Brain pulse 3 — prevent gap |\n144: | 23:00 | 2–3R + (4+4)T | Brain pulse 4 + bedtime basal |\n145: \n146: **Total target: ~10–12u T basal + ~15–20u R bolus = 25–32u daily**\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## Pre-Bed Correction Rule\n151: \n152: > **Never dose for current BG — dose for projected BG at insulin peak**\n153: \n154: **Formula:** Projected BG = Current BG + (rising rate × time to insulin peak)\n155: \n156: - H (Humalog): peak at ~0.5–1.5h (from 2u to 5u+)\n157: - R (Regular): peak at ~1–3h (from 3u to 6u+)\n158: - Rising trajectory → dose more aggressively than number suggests\n159: - Falling trajectory → dose more conservatively\n160: - Example: fat-only evening + (4+4)T basal = safe environment for 2–2½H correction at projected 135–140\n161: \n162: Pre-bed R essential for sleep quality and mental clarity — independent of BG control — via brain insulin pulsatility mechanism.\n163: \n164: ---\n165: \n166: ## Tresiba (T) Rules\n167: \n168: - **4T single site < 8h effective coverage — always split (4+4)T or similar**\n169: - Never use single-site 7T overnight — caused multiple 37–53 mg/dL morning lows\n170: \n171: | Day type | Protocol |\n172: |---|---|\n173: | Eating day | Afternoon (4–5T) + bedtime (4–5T) split |\n174: | Fasting day | Skip or reduce morning T by 1–2u; bedtime (4+4)T only |\n175: \n176: ---\n177: \n178: ## NPH / Insulatard (I) Rules\n179: \n180: **⚠️ Your compressed I duration is 4–7h (textbook says 12–18h). It behaves like 1.5x potent R.**\n181: \n182: | Starting BG | Maximum safe I dose |\n183: |---|---|\n184: | <1