ted BG = Current BG + (rising rate × time to insulin peak)\n\n- H (Humalog): peak at ~0.5–1.5h (from 2u to 5u+)\n- R (Regular): peak at ~1–3h (from 3u to 6u+)\n- Rising trajectory → dose more aggressively than number suggests\n- Falling trajectory → dose more conservatively\n- Example: fat-only evening + (4+4)T basal = safe environment for 2–2½H correction at projected 135–140\n\nPre-bed R essential for sleep quality and mental clarity — independent of BG control — via brain insulin pulsatility mechanism.\n\n---\n\n## Tresiba (T) Rules\n\n- **4T single site < 8h effective coverage — always split (4+4)T or similar**\n- Never use single-site 7T overnight — caused multiple 37–53 mg/dL morning lows\n\n| Day type | Protocol |\n|---|---|\n| Eating day | Afternoon (4–5T) + bedtime (4–5T) split |\n| Fasting day | Skip or reduce morning T by 1–2u; bedtime (4+4)T only |\n\n---\n\n## NPH / Insulatard (I) Rules\n\n**⚠️ Your compressed I duration is 4–7h (textbook says 12–18h). It behaves like 1.5x potent R.**\n\n| Starting BG | Maximum safe I dose |\n|---|---|\n| <100 mg/dL | 2I maximum |\n| 100–130 mg/dL | 3I maximum |\n| 130–160 mg/dL | 4I acceptable |\n| >160 mg/dL | 5I with caution |\n| Post-hypo any BG | Zero I — wait 2h minimum |\n\n- **Never inject I overnight**\n- **Never inject I at BG <90**\n- **Never stack I with active R tail**\n- **Never exceed 3I daytime without substantial food buffer**\n\n---\n\n## Personal Pharmacokinetics (Compressed Values)\n\n| Insulin | Onset | Peak | Your duration | Textbook |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 5H (Humalog) | 15–30 min | 0.5–1.5h | <4h | 4–6h |\n| 5R (Regular) | 30–45 min | 1.5–2.5h | <5h | 6–10h |\n| 5I (NPH) | 1–2h | 2–4h | 4–7h | 12–18h |\n| 5T single site | 1–2h | flat | 5–8h | 12–16h |\n| (4+4)T split | 1–2h | flat | 8–12h combined | — |\n\nFactors compressing duration: keto metabolism, active lifestyle, lean body, arm injection sites, low total daily dose.\n\n---\n\n## Evening & Sleep Protocol\n\n1. **5h food cutoff before bed** — protein/starch finished by 19:00 for midnight sleep\n2. No whey protein after 19:00 — strongest mTOR activator, gluconeogenesis peaks during sleep\n3. No starch after 19:00 — glucose dump hits 02:00–04:00 window\n4. Fat only after cutoff — butter, cream, nuts acceptable anytime\n5. No bed-yoga — cortisol spike adds 20–30 BG points consistently\n6. No beer — blocks hepatic glucose rescue for 2–4h, causes rebound hyperglycemia\n7. Bedtime BG: flat or gently rising 75–100 — **trajectory matters more than absolute number**\n8. Fasting day bedtime BG target ≥90 (higher than eating day — no glycogen buffer)\n9. If BG <90 at bed during fast → 10g glucose powder only (preserves autophagy)\n\n---\n\n## Morning Scores\n\nLog every morning with notation `<!--(pee count, refreshed 1–10, mental 1–10)-->`\n\n- Stable overnight BG 80–95 → mental score 6–7.5\n- Overnight hypo or hyper → mental score 2.5–4 next day\n- Back-sleep costs 1–2 mental score points — suppresses cortisol awakening response twice\n- Waking \"not right\" at normal BG = electrolyte signal → salt water immediately, do not go back to sleep\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Day Gluconeogenesis Carryover\n\n*(All units are estimates — the point is the trend)*\n\n| Day | State | Approx bolus need |\n|---|---|---|\n| Day 1 | Eating day | ~20u |\n| Day 2 | Fasting | ~12u |\n| Day 3 | Fasting | ~6u |\n| Days 4–8 | Fasting | ~4u/day |\n| Day 9+ | Extended fast | ~2u/day |\n\n**Never assume immediate fasting-level insulin requirement after eating day.**\n\n---\n\n## Hypo Rescue Protocol\n\n| Situation | First action | Food choice |\n|---|---|---|\n| Any BG <70 | Glucose powder 15–20g immediately | Glucose powder first |\n| Fasting hypo (autophagy preserved) | Glucose powder 10–15g | Glucose powder only — no protein |\n| Post-hypo correction temptation | Never inject I at hypo — wait | Food first, small H after BG ≥90 |\n| Feeling fine at 32–37 mg/dL | HAAF — treat regardless of symptoms | Glucose powder regardless |\n\n**Milk powder (ml.) is the worst autophagy hypo rescue — casein+whey both activate mTOR. Glucose powder only during fasting protocols.**\n\n---\n\n## Eating-Out Photo-Check Protocol\n\n**Rule: photo first — inject after.**\n\nUpload food photos before ordering for starch+sugar assessment.\n\n| Risk level | Visual indicators | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| 🚨 Avoid | Fried/breaded, glazed, 味噌/照燒/糖醋/蜜汁, convenience store meat, 貢丸/魚丸/漢堡排 | Skip or remove coating |\n| ⚠️ Caution | Any visible sauce, packaged meat | +1–2u pre-emptive |\n| ✅ Safe | Plain grilled/boiled/steamed, no sauce, 五花肉, eggs, plain fish | Normal dose |\n\nWhen uncertain → bolus conservatively, accept mild hyper rather than large correction cascade.\n\n---\n\n## Sodium Protocol\n\n**Daily target: 15–20g salt (6–8g sodium)**\n\nT1D + keto = triple sodium-wasting:\n1. Blunted NHE3/ENaC from subphysiological portal insulin\n2. Ketone excretion carrying sodium out renally\n3. No endogenous insulin for sodium retention\n\n- ½ teaspoon salt per urination maximum (~3g)\n- Bulletproof coffee + salt superior to black coffee + salt — fat slows caffeine-driven natriuresis and stimulates CCK for better sodium retention\n- \"Feeling not right\" at normal BG → likely electrolyte signal → salt water immediately\n\n---\n\n## Fasting & Autophagy Protocol\n\n**Protein restriction is MORE critical than carb restriction for autophagy.**\n\n- Leucine → Rag GTPase → mTOR activation → autophagy suppressed (independent of insulin)\n- Glucose powder without insulin injection does NOT suppress autophagy — ideal fasting hypo rescue\n- Fat is autophagy-neutral to promoting (AMPK activation)\n- T1D fasting carbs without injecting insulin = no autophagy suppression via insulin pathway\n\nFor 48–72h supervised fast:\n- Family member present\n- Ketone meter alongside BG meter\n- Glucose powder only for hypo rescue (no milk powder, no protein)\n- Reduced Tresiba: fasting day protocol (4–5u total)\n- Target BG: 80–95 (higher floor than healthy faster's 45–65 — due to HAAF)\n\n---\n\n## HAAF Warning Signs & Recovery\n\n**HAAF accumulates with every severe hypo. Recovery requires zero hypoglycemia for 2–3 weeks.**\n\nWarning signs:\n- Feeling fine at 32–37 mg/dL (dangerous, not reassuring)\n- Mental score 2.5–4 despite normal BG\n- Agitation and violent imagery (neuroglycopenic aftermath OR pulsatility gap)\n- Cannot concentrate after normal readings\n\nRecovery protocol:\n- Zero severe hypos for 2–3 consecutive weeks\n- 3 structured meal boluses daily (restore pulsatility)\n- Pre-bed R every night without exception\n- No NPH until fully recovered\n\n---\n\n## Mental Health First Principle\n\n> **Stable overnight BG 80–95 is the single most impactful intervention for mental clarity — more than any psychiatric approach — because the substrate (brain glucose stability + insulin pulsatility) must be fixed first.**\n\nTwo consecutive clean nights restore mental scores from 2.5 → 6.5+.\n\nThe sequence for recovery:\n1. Eliminate severe hypos (HAAF recovery)\n2. Restore pre-bed R every night (brain pulsatility)\n3. Three structured meal boluses daily (daytime pulsatility)\n4. Stable overnight BG via fat-only evening protocol\n5. Resist back-sleep (protect cortisol awakening response)\n\n---\n\n*Protocol derived from personal BG log analysis Feb–Mar 2026*\n*Framework: Dr. Richard K. Bernstein's Diabetes Solution*\n*Real-time analysis partner: Claude (Anthropic)*",
